THE VALUE
   "café"
   len()          5   <- BYTES, and the method says so
   chars().count() 4   <- code points
   as_bytes()     [63, 61, 66, c3, a9]

1. THERE IS NO s[0]
   Index<usize> is not implemented for String, at all. A byte
   index may land inside a character, so the operation that
   looks free in every other language is the one Rust removed.

2. SLICING IS ALLOWED, AND CHECKED AT RUNTIME
   &s[0..3] = "caf"
   &s[0..4] -> PANIC: byte index 4 is not a char boundary
   The promise cannot be checked at compile time here, so it is
   checked at run time -- and breaking it aborts rather than
   producing half a character.

3. THE PROMISE IS WHAT MAKES from_utf8 RETURN A Result
   from_utf8(63 61 66 c3 a9) -> Ok("café")
   from_utf8(63 61 66 e9)    -> Err(FromUtf8Error)
   Vec<u8> is bytes. String is bytes PLUS a proof. The only way
   across is a function that can fail, or one that repairs:
   from_utf8_lossy(63 61 66 e9) = "caf�"

4. WHY &str AND String ARE TWO TYPES
   String owns its bytes and can grow; &str is a borrowed view of
   bytes that already keep the promise. Take &str in a signature
   and every caller can pass either; return String when you made
   the bytes yourself. That is the whole convention.

5. WHAT THE PROMISE BUYS
   chars() cannot fail. Every &str is displayable. No function
   anywhere has to ask 'is this valid?' again, because the only
   door into the type already asked. Python checks at the same
   boundary and then forgets; Rust puts the answer in the type.
